The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
err = rtnl_configure_link(dev, ifm);
if (err < 0) {
    unregister_netdevice(dev);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when `rtnl_configure_link()` fails, the original code directly calls `unregister_netdevice(dev)`, which does not account for the possibility of needing to handle the device unregistration differently if `dev_change_net_namespace()` later fails. This can lead to improper cleanup and resource leaks.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (err < 0)
    goto out_unregister;
...
out_unregister:
    if (ops->newlink) {
        LIST_HEAD(list_kill);
        ops->dellink(dev, &list_kill);
        unregister_netdevice_many(&list_kill);
    } else {
        unregister_netdevice(dev);
    }
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a new label `out_unregister` that centralizes the unregistration logic. This ensures that if either `rtnl_configure_link()` or `dev_change_net_namespace()` fails, the appropriate unregistration method is called, preventing resource leaks and ensuring consistent device states.

